"use strict";(self.webpackChunkdatabricks_monolith=self.webpackChunkdatabricks_monolith||[]).push([[73297],{573297:e=>{e.exports='# Oracle Packages\n\nOracle `CREATE PACKAGE` / `CREATE PACKAGE BODY` migration to DBSQL. Covers the spec/body split, package-level state, overloading, package types, initialization blocks, private members, and pragmas.\n\n> For the procedure and function bodies _inside_ a package, load `oracle/04-procedures-variables.md` — that file covers stored procedure syntax, UDF syntax, cursors, exception handling, and variables. This file covers only the package-as-container concerns.\n\n## ORACLE\n\n### Packages — no direct equivalent\n\nOracle **packages** (`CREATE PACKAGE ... CREATE PACKAGE BODY ...`) have no direct DBSQL equivalent. DBSQL has flat procedures and functions scoped by `catalog.schema`, with no grouping container, no shared session state, and no overloading.\n\n**Translation approach:**\n\n1. **The spec contains two kinds of declarations — handle them differently. Do not blanket-drop the spec.**\n   - **Procedure / function declarations** (`PROCEDURE foo(...);`, `FUNCTION foo(...) RETURN ...;`) — drop them. They are signatures only with no behavior; the body restates them in full. Use them as a _checklist_ of which body members are public.\n   - **Variable, constant, cursor, type, and exception declarations** in the spec — **migrate them**. These are real public-state / public-type declarations that introduce names the body\'s procedures and external callers reference, and have no DBSQL equivalent without explicit translation:\n     - `g_some_var TYPE [:= default];` (public package variable) → no shared session state in DBSQL. Re-target per "Package-level state" below (staging table, OUT/INOUT param, or caller-threaded session variable).\n     - `c_const CONSTANT TYPE := value;` (public constant) → zero-arg function or inline literal at every reference site, per "Package-level state" \xa7Package constants below.\n     - `CURSOR <name> IS SELECT ... ;` (public cursor) → no shared cursor object in DBSQL. Wrap the underlying SELECT as a view (preferred when reused by many callers) or inline it into each procedure that uses it. See "Package-level state" below.\n     - `TYPE <name> IS RECORD (...);` / `TABLE OF ...` / `REF CURSOR;` (public type) → replicate as `STRUCT<...>` / `ARRAY<STRUCT<...>>` / `RETURNS TABLE(...)` at every parameter and return site. See "Package types" below.\n     - `<name> EXCEPTION;` (often paired with `PRAGMA EXCEPTION_INIT`) → map to `SIGNAL SQLSTATE \'45000\' SET MESSAGE_TEXT = ...`. See `oracle/04b-error-handling.md`.\n   - The body\'s `CREATE PACKAGE BODY ... END;` is migrated separately via steps 2–7 below — those steps deal with body-level concerns (private members, init blocks, body-only state) and are unchanged by this spec/body split.\n2. **Each procedure / function in the body becomes a standalone DBSQL object**, named with a package-name prefix to preserve grouping and avoid collisions across packages:\n   - `order_pkg.place_order` → `my_catalog.my_schema.order_pkg_place_order`\n   - `order_pkg.total_for_customer` → `my_catalog.my_schema.order_pkg_total_for_customer`\n3. **Package-level variables / cursors / constants** have no equivalent — each DBSQL call is independent. See "Package-level state" below.\n4. **Overloading** (multiple package members with the same name) — DBSQL does not allow overloading. See "Overloading inside packages" below.\n5. **Private members** (declared in body but not spec) become regular DBSQL objects — DBSQL has no public/private distinction. Use `GRANT` / `REVOKE` to restrict access if needed.\n6. **Initialization blocks** (`BEGIN ... END` at the end of the body) have no equivalent — extract to an explicit init procedure that callers invoke. See "Initialization blocks" below.\n7. **Forward declarations**, `PRAGMA SERIALLY_REUSABLE`, `PRAGMA RESTRICT_REFERENCES`, etc. — drop them. They have no DBSQL meaning.\n\n### Worked example — `order_pkg`\n\n```sql\n-- Oracle package\nCREATE OR REPLACE PACKAGE order_pkg AS\n    PROCEDURE place_order(p_customer IN NUMBER, p_amount IN NUMBER);\n    FUNCTION  total_for_customer(p_customer IN NUMBER) RETURN NUMBER;\nEND order_pkg;\n/\n\nCREATE OR REPLACE PACKAGE BODY order_pkg AS\n    g_last_order_id NUMBER;  -- package-level state: NO direct DBSQL equivalent\n\n    PROCEDURE place_order(p_customer IN NUMBER, p_amount IN NUMBER) AS BEGIN\n        INSERT INTO orders (customer_id, amount) VALUES (p_customer, p_amount)\n            RETURNING order_id INTO g_last_order_id;\n    END;\n\n    FUNCTION total_for_customer(p_customer IN NUMBER) RETURN NUMBER AS\n        v_total NUMBER;\n    BEGIN\n        SELECT SUM(amount) INTO v_total FROM orders WHERE customer_id = p_customer;\n        RETURN v_total;\n    END;\nEND order_pkg;\n/\n\n-- DBSQL: two standalone objects + state moved to a staging table or parameter\nCREATE OR REPLACE PROCEDURE my_catalog.my_schema.order_pkg_place_order(\n    IN p_customer INT, IN p_amount DECIMAL(18, 2)\n)\nLANGUAGE SQL\nSQL SECURITY INVOKER\nAS BEGIN\n    INSERT INTO my_catalog.my_schema.orders (customer_id, amount) VALUES (p_customer, p_amount);\n    -- NOTE: Oracle package variable g_last_order_id has no DBSQL equivalent. Callers that\n    -- need the inserted id must query `SELECT MAX(order_id) FROM orders WHERE customer_id = p_customer`\n    -- or use a UUID generated before insert (safer under concurrency).\nEND;\n\nCREATE OR REPLACE FUNCTION my_catalog.my_schema.order_pkg_total_for_customer(p_customer INT)\nRETURNS DECIMAL(18, 2)\nRETURN (SELECT SUM(amount) FROM my_catalog.my_schema.orders WHERE customer_id = p_customer);\n```\n\n> **CRITICAL — Oracle `FUNCTION` with an `EXCEPTION` handler that raises must become a DBSQL `PROCEDURE`, not a `FUNCTION`.** DBSQL functions (both SQL UDFs and table UDFs) **cannot execute `SIGNAL`** — they have no procedural body in which a raise can occur. Any Oracle `FUNCTION foo(...) RETURN <type> IS ... EXCEPTION WHEN <cond> THEN RAISE <user_exc>; END;` must therefore translate to a `CREATE OR REPLACE PROCEDURE` that returns its result via a result-set `SELECT` (or `OUT` parameter), with the `RAISE` rewritten as `SIGNAL SQLSTATE \'45XYZ\' SET MESSAGE_TEXT = ...` per `oracle/04b-error-handling.md`. The decision matrix:\n>\n> | Oracle source                                                                                  | DBSQL target                                                                                                                                                                                         |\n> | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n> | `FUNCTION` with **no** `EXCEPTION` block                                                       | `CREATE FUNCTION ... RETURNS <type>` (scalar) or `RETURNS TABLE (...)` (set-returning). Direct.                                                                                                      |\n> | `FUNCTION` with `EXCEPTION` that **only swallows**                                             | `CREATE FUNCTION` — translate the swallow into `COALESCE`, `IFNULL`, or a `CASE` guard inside the `RETURN` expr.                                                                                     |\n> | `FUNCTION` with `EXCEPTION` that **raises** (e.g. `RAISE NO_DATA_FOUND` or `RAISE <user_exc>`) | **`CREATE PROCEDURE`** — only procedures can `SIGNAL`. Caller-side switches from `v := f(x)` / `SELECT f(x)` to `CALL ... ;` consuming a result set. Flag the call-site rewrite as a migration TODO. |\n>\n> Silently downgrading a raising `FUNCTION` to a non-raising `FUNCTION` (returning `NULL` on the missing case) is a behavioral change that callers depending on the exception will not notice until the wrong-row codepath fires in production.\n\n### Package-level state\n\nOracle package variables persist for the **duration of a session** — every procedure in the package sees the same value, and the value survives between calls within one session. DBSQL has no equivalent: every procedure call is independent, and there is no per-session state shared across objects.\n\n**Drift trap — silent state loss:**\n\n```sql\n-- Oracle: caller relies on package state across two calls\norder_pkg.place_order(42, 100);                    -- writes g_last_order_id\nSELECT order_pkg.get_last_order_id() FROM DUAL;    -- reads g_last_order_id\n\n-- A naive DBSQL translation that "preserves the API" silently breaks:\nCALL my_catalog.my_schema.order_pkg_place_order(42, 100);\nSELECT my_catalog.my_schema.order_pkg_get_last_order_id();   -- returns NULL or stale value\n```\n\nThe two DBSQL calls do not share state. Without a deliberate state-passing rewrite, the second call has no way to see what the first call did.\n\n**Translation patterns (in order of preference):**\n\n| Pattern                                   | When to use                                                                                                                                            |\n| ----------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ |\n| **OUT / INOUT parameter**                 | State is produced by one call and consumed by the immediate next call (within the same caller). Preferred — fully explicit.                            |\n| **Return as result**                      | State is read-only output of a single call (e.g. `get_last_order_id` collapses into the `place_order` return). Preferred when the API can be reshaped. |\n| **Staging table**                         | State must persist across many calls or across sessions. Add a session/correlation key column to avoid concurrent-caller collision.                    |\n| **Session variable** (`DECLARE VARIABLE`) | State is orchestrated by a single caller across multiple calls in one session — caller declares the variable and threads it through CALL parameters.   |\n\nThe package\'s API shape almost always has to change. Flag this in a migration TODO so the caller side is reviewed.\n\n**Package constants** (`c_max_retries CONSTANT NUMBER := 3;`) are a special case — the value never changes, so there is no concurrency hazard. Translate to either:\n\n```sql\n-- Option A: a zero-arg function (referenceable from any UDF/procedure body)\nCREATE OR REPLACE FUNCTION my_catalog.my_schema.order_pkg_c_max_retries()\nRETURNS INT\nRETURN 3;\n\n-- Option B: inline the literal at every reference site (preferred when the constant is small\n-- and unlikely to change; avoids the function-call overhead for a literal).\n```\n\n**Package-level cursors** (cursors declared in the spec/body and reused across procedures) — there is no shared cursor object in DBSQL. The translation depends on whether the cursor takes parameters:\n\n- **Unparameterized cursor** (`CURSOR c_active_orders IS SELECT ... WHERE status=\'ACTIVE\';`) → wrap as a **view** (preferred when reused across many callers, since the query plan is shared) or inline the SELECT into each procedure that uses it.\n- **Parameterized cursor** (`CURSOR c_orders_by_customer (p_customer_id NUMBER) IS SELECT ... WHERE customer_id = p_customer_id;`) → wrap as a **table-valued function (TVF)**. DBSQL views do not accept parameters, so a view is **not** an option here. Callers replace `OPEN <pkg>.c_orders_by_customer(N)` with `SELECT * FROM <pkg>_c_orders_by_customer(N);`.\n\n```sql\n-- Oracle: parameterized cursor in a package spec\nCURSOR c_orders_by_customer (p_customer_id NUMBER) IS\n    SELECT order_id, status, total_amount FROM orders WHERE customer_id = p_customer_id;\n\n-- DBSQL: TVF preserves the parameterized query shape — and the public spec name.\n-- Note: RETURNS TABLE column types match the underlying orders table exactly\n-- (total_amount is DECIMAL(12, 2) in the schema, not the generic DECIMAL(18, 2) widest-fit).\n-- See oracle/04-procedures-variables.md "match the column type at API boundaries".\nCREATE OR REPLACE FUNCTION my_catalog.my_schema.order_pkg_c_orders_by_customer(p_customer_id BIGINT)\nRETURNS TABLE (order_id BIGINT, status STRING, total_amount DECIMAL(12, 2))\nRETURN\n    SELECT order_id, status, total_amount\n    FROM my_catalog.my_schema.orders\n    WHERE customer_id = p_customer_id;\n```\n\nCursors declared in the **spec** are public — external callers may `OPEN` them — so they must be migrated to a callable name even if the body never reads them. A spec-level cursor reduced to a code comment is a silent breakage of the package\'s public surface.\n\n### Overloading inside packages\n\nPackage overloading is the _single most common source of silent bugs_ when migrating from Oracle, because DBSQL allows `CREATE OR REPLACE FUNCTION` with the same name and different signatures to **silently replace** the previous function — there is no error, the second `CREATE` just overwrites the first.\n\n```sql\n-- Oracle package with overloads\nCREATE OR REPLACE PACKAGE fmt_pkg AS\n    FUNCTION format_amount(p_amt NUMBER) RETURN VARCHAR2;\n    FUNCTION format_amount(p_amt NUMBER, p_currency VARCHAR2) RETURN VARCHAR2;\n    FUNCTION format_amount(p_amt VARCHAR2) RETURN VARCHAR2;  -- string overload\nEND fmt_pkg;\n/\n```\n\n**Resolution at call sites:** Oracle picks the overload by argument types. After migration, every call site needs to be reviewed — even when the overloads are unified into a single DBSQL function with `DEFAULT` parameters, the caller\'s expression types must be checked (e.g. the `VARCHAR2` overload silently invoking `TO_CHAR` differs from the `NUMBER` overload).\n\n**Translation options:**\n\n| Option                                       | When to use                                                                                                                                                                   |\n| -------------------------------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| **Rename with signature suffix**             | Most reliable. `format_amount_n`, `format_amount_n_c`, `format_amount_s`. Every call site must change — that is the point: it forces review.                                  |\n| **Unify with `DEFAULT` and a discriminator** | When all overloads share return type and the parameter sets nest cleanly. See the "Function overloading" section in `oracle/04-procedures-variables.md` for the unified form. |\n| **Unify by widening to STRING**              | When a numeric and a string overload exist, accept `STRING` and parse internally. Risks losing type-safety — flag with a TODO.                                                |\n\n> See the **"Function overloading"** subsection in `oracle/04-procedures-variables.md` for the worked unified-with-DEFAULT example. The package context adds the call-site-resolution review step to that pattern.\n\n### Package types (`RECORD`, `TABLE OF`, `REF CURSOR`)\n\nOracle packages can declare types in the spec that are then used as parameter / return types across procedures in the package — and externally by other PL/SQL clients. DBSQL has no shared user-defined type system.\n\n| Oracle package type                                          | DBSQL translation                                                                                                                                                                                                         |\n| ------------------------------------------------------------ | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `TYPE order_rec IS RECORD (id NUMBER, amount NUMBER);`       | `STRUCT<id: INT, amount: DECIMAL(18,2)>` at each parameter / return site. No shared type — duplicated at every reference. See `oracle/03-data-types.md`.                                                                  |\n| `TYPE order_tab IS TABLE OF order_rec INDEX BY PLS_INTEGER;` | `ARRAY<STRUCT<...>>` for procedure parameters; for cross-procedure data flow, prefer a temporary view or `RETURNS TABLE(...)` table UDF. See `oracle/05-temp-tables-ctes.md` for collection mappings.                     |\n| `TYPE order_cursor IS REF CURSOR;`                           | No equivalent. Procedures return a result set via the last `SELECT` statement (notebook context) or `RETURNS TABLE` (SQL warehouse). See "REF CURSOR / SYS_REFCURSOR result sets" in `oracle/04-procedures-variables.md`. |\n\nPractical consequence: if procedure A in a package returned a `TABLE OF order_rec` consumed by procedure B in the same package, the DBSQL versions must communicate via a real (or temporary) table that both reference — there is no in-memory typed-collection handoff.\n\n### Initialization blocks\n\nOracle package bodies may end with a `BEGIN ... END;` block that runs once per session, on first reference to any package member. Common uses: lazy table creation, session-context setup, cache warming.\n\n```sql\n-- Oracle: package init block\nCREATE OR REPLACE PACKAGE BODY my_pkg AS\n    g_initialized BOOLEAN := FALSE;\n    PROCEDURE foo AS BEGIN ... END;\nBEGIN\n    -- runs once per session on first reference\n    SELECT some_setting INTO g_some_setting FROM config;\n    g_initialized := TRUE;\nEND my_pkg;\n/\n```\n\nDBSQL has no per-session-on-first-reference hook. Translation:\n\n1. Extract the init logic into an explicit procedure: `CREATE PROCEDURE my_pkg_init(...)`.\n2. Document that callers must invoke it once at session start.\n3. If the original package wrote into package state, the init procedure has nowhere to write — re-target to a session variable, a staging table, or fold the init read-side into each procedure that needed the value.\n\nIf the init logic is **order-dependent** (must run before any other call) and the caller cannot be modified, flag with a TODO — there is no way to enforce ordering from the function/procedure side in DBSQL.\n\n### Private members\n\nOracle packages distinguish public (declared in spec) from private (declared in body only) members. DBSQL has no such distinction — every procedure / function is reachable by anyone with `EXECUTE` privilege.\n\nTranslation: create both as regular DBSQL objects. To preserve "private" semantics, use:\n\n```sql\nREVOKE EXECUTE ON FUNCTION my_catalog.my_schema.order_pkg_internal_helper FROM `account users`;\nGRANT  EXECUTE ON FUNCTION my_catalog.my_schema.order_pkg_internal_helper TO   `<owning service principal>`;\n```\n\nA naming convention also helps signal intent — e.g. `_internal` suffix on what was private.\n\n### Cross-package calls\n\nInside a package body, calls to other package members are unqualified (`place_order(...)` resolves to `order_pkg.place_order` because the call sits inside `order_pkg`\'s body). After flattening, every reference must be requalified at the new top-level name:\n\n```sql\n-- Oracle (inside order_pkg.body): unqualified call resolves to same package\nCREATE OR REPLACE PACKAGE BODY order_pkg AS\n    PROCEDURE place_order(...) AS BEGIN\n        validate_customer(p_customer);                 -- order_pkg.validate_customer\n        billing_pkg.charge_card(p_customer, p_amount); -- explicit cross-package\n    END;\nEND order_pkg;\n/\n\n-- DBSQL: every call is fully qualified at the new flattened name\nCREATE OR REPLACE PROCEDURE my_catalog.my_schema.order_pkg_place_order(...)\nLANGUAGE SQL\nSQL SECURITY INVOKER\nAS BEGIN\n    CALL my_catalog.my_schema.order_pkg_validate_customer(p_customer);\n    CALL my_catalog.my_schema.billing_pkg_charge_card(p_customer, p_amount);\nEND;\n```\n\n**Same-package private helpers** that were unqualified in Oracle become the most error-prone — easy to miss during translation because there is no syntactic marker. Grep the original body for every identifier that matches a peer member name and explicitly rewrite each call.\n\n**Mutually recursive package members** — Oracle resolves these at body-compile time. DBSQL `CREATE OR REPLACE` is single-statement and does not support self-reference in SQL UDF bodies, but **does** support mutual recursion across procedures (the called procedure is resolved at call time, not creation time). Order the `CREATE OR REPLACE` statements so each procedure exists before the first caller runs; if A calls B and B calls A, create stub bodies first then replace each with the real body, or use a Python UDF if recursive SQL UDFs are required.\n\n### Package management DDL\n\n| Oracle                                                                                                      | DBSQL                                                                                                        | Notes                                                                                                                                                                            |\n| ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ | -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `ALTER PACKAGE <name> COMPILE [SPECIFICATION \\| BODY \\| PACKAGE] [REUSE SETTINGS] [<plsql_param>=<value>];` | Drop                                                                                                         | Recompiles the spec or body. DBSQL has no separate compile step — every `CREATE OR REPLACE PROCEDURE / FUNCTION` is also a compile.                                              |\n| `DROP PACKAGE <name>;`                                                                                      | `DROP PROCEDURE [IF EXISTS] my_catalog.my_schema.<pkg>_<member>;` (one per member) + `DROP FUNCTION` per UDF | DBSQL has no package container, so the cleanup script must list every flattened member by name. A naming convention (`<pkg>_<member>`) makes this scriptable.                    |\n| `DROP PACKAGE BODY <name>;`                                                                                 | Drop                                                                                                         | Body-only drop in Oracle leaves the spec intact (so callers compile but execution fails). DBSQL has no equivalent — replace each member\'s body via `CREATE OR REPLACE` to match. |\n| `ALTER PACKAGE <name> RENAME TO <new>;`                                                                     | Re-issue all `CREATE OR REPLACE PROCEDURE / FUNCTION` with the new prefix and drop the old set               | DBSQL cannot rename procedures or functions in place.                                                                                                                            |\n| `ALTER SESSION SET PLSQL_OPTIMIZE_LEVEL = <0..3>;`                                                          | Drop                                                                                                         | Session-level optimizer hint. DBSQL applies its own optimizer settings.                                                                                                          |\n| `ALTER SESSION SET PLSQL_WARNINGS = \'<level>\';`                                                             | Drop                                                                                                         | Session-level warning verbosity. DBSQL has no equivalent.                                                                                                                        |\n| `ALTER SESSION SET PLSQL_CCFLAGS = \'<flag>:<value>, ...\';`                                                  | Drop after applying conditional-compilation rewrites                                                         | See `oracle/09-scripting-control-flow.md` for the `$IF` / `$$flag` rewrite. Drop the session setting once the directives have been resolved at translation time.                 |\n| `ALTER SESSION SET PLSQL_CODE_TYPE = \'NATIVE\' \\| \'INTERPRETED\';`                                            | Drop                                                                                                         | Native compilation is Oracle-specific.                                                                                                                                           |\n| `SHOW ERRORS PACKAGE [BODY] <name>;`                                                                        | Drop                                                                                                         | SQL\\*Plus introspection — see `00-quirks.md`.                                                                                                                                    |\n\n### Forward declarations and pragmas\n\n- **Forward declarations** (`PROCEDURE foo;` in the body before its definition) — drop them. DBSQL has no concept of forward declarations; the second `CREATE OR REPLACE` simply replaces the first. Ordering matters only at runtime, not at creation time.\n- **`PRAGMA SERIALLY_REUSABLE` (package level)** — drop. There is no per-session reuse semantic in DBSQL. The pragma\'s effect (resetting package state between top-level calls) is moot once package state has been re-targeted to a staging table or session variable per "Package-level state" above.\n- **`PRAGMA RESTRICT_REFERENCES(<name>, WNDS, RNDS, WNPS, RNPS [, TRUST])`** — drop. Purity declarations are inferred by DBSQL.\n- **`PRAGMA EXCEPTION_INIT(my_exc, -20001)`** — drop the pragma. The named exception itself becomes a `SIGNAL SQLSTATE \'45000\' SET MESSAGE_TEXT = ...` per the exception mapping table in `oracle/04b-error-handling.md`.\n- **`PRAGMA INLINE`**, **`PRAGMA UDF`**, **`PRAGMA COVERAGE`**, **`PRAGMA DEPRECATE`** — drop. See the subprogram-level pragma table in `oracle/04-procedures-variables.md` for the full catalog.\n'}}]);
